  240:   `BlockGeometryRegistry._clampToNearest` does not jump to an unrelated block.
  241: - Cross-block selection endpoints stay addressable for word/paragraph selection.
  242: 
  243: Keep-alive is bounded: at most the caret + 2 endpoints (usually 1-2 ids after
  244: dedup), so memory does not grow with document size the way full mounting did.
  245: 
  246: ### Known boundaries (acceptable degradation, not crashes)
  247: 
  248: - **Drag into a far, never-built region.** The gesture overlay's
  249:   `positionFromGlobalOffset` only knows mounted blocks. Dragging the pointer
  250:   into a region that has never been built resolves to the nearest mounted block
  251:   (via `_clampToNearest`), which may be the keep-alive caret/endpoint or the
  252:   visible window. The selection model itself stays correct; only the *visual*
  253:   clamp target degrades until the region scrolls into view.
  254: - **Auto-scroll-on-drag (B2).** `SelectionGestureOverlay` drives scroll on two
  255:   layers. (1) **Synchronous edge scroll** runs on every pointer-move for every
  256:   device: when the pointer is within the 48px top/bottom edge band, the scroll
  257:   offset jumps one step toward the edge. This is the primary scroll driver under
  258:   a drag — the scrollable's own pan recognizer does not win the gesture arena
  259:   beneath the overlay's `Listener`, so without this the list would not follow a
  260:   touch drag at all. (2) **Continuous ticker** (mouse/stylus only): when the
  261:   pointer is held still near an edge, a `Ticker` advances the offset every frame
  262:   so the user can scroll past the visible area without moving the pointer. After
  263:   each ticker-driven scroll, the overlay re-resolves the extent at the last
  264:   pointer coordinate so the visible selection continues growing under a
  265:   stationary pointer. Range selections produced this way do not trigger the
  266:   editor's caret-scroll-into-view path, avoiding a fight between the two scroll
  267:   drivers. Touch does not engage the ticker — touch selection past the viewport
  268:   is reserved for dedicated handles (C9).
  269: - **Auto-scroll-to-caret for far jumps.** When a programmatic selection
  270:   change moves the caret to a block that is not currently built (e.g. jumping
  271:   to block 400 in a 1000-block doc while viewing block 0), the editor
  272:   estimates the target scroll offset from the average mounted-block height,
  273:   jumps the viewport close enough for `ListView` to build the block, then a
  274:   follow-up frame pixel-aligns so the caret sits inside the viewport. This
  275:   avoids a `ScrollablePositionedList` dependency. The estimate degrades when
  276:   block heights vary wildly (mixed media / code blocks); in that case the
  277:   follow-up frame still corrects to the true position. Typing and nearby taps
  278:   are no-ops because the caret is already in view.
  279: - **`TextPainter` cache** lives per `_TextSelectionSurface`; a virtualised-away
  280:   block drops its cache and re-measures on remount.
  281: 
  282: ### Selection correctness across virtualised regions
  283: 
  284: `DocumentSelection` is block-index based and `DeleteSelectionCommand` /
  285: `_selectionRangeForPath` operate over block-index ranges, not over mounted
  286: widgets. So a selection spanning many unmounted interior blocks is still valid:
  287: the model holds it; deleting it works; only the *highlight* for the unmounted
  288: interior blocks is not painted (they are off-screen anyway).
